From: MacGameVideoFix <https://github.com/MathiasKowoll/MacGameVideoFix>
Subject: [PATCH] winegstreamer: 2D-capable buffers from the media source too

WHERE THIS CAME FROM

This patch belongs to MacGameVideoFix's own series, `mgvf-NNNN`, which is kept
separate from winevideo's precisely so that neither is mistaken for the other.
It is applied alongside theirs and after them, but it is not one of theirs. It was written on 2026-08-31 in
MacGameVideoFix, from reading winegstreamer's own source, after a title crashed
in a way winevideo's 0008 describes exactly but does not reach. It is offered
here so that winevideo, Procyon, or anyone else carrying a patched winegstreamer
can take it if they want it; nothing in it is specific to this project.

THE FAULT

An application asks a decoded video frame for IMF2DBuffer2, is told the
interface does not exist, and dereferences the NULL it was handed. Measured in
METAL GEAR SOLID: Peace Walker (Master Collection) on wine-11.0-8726-g2e2f5fca349,
x86_64, macOS on Apple Silicon:

    EXCEPTION 0xc0000005 at 0x1400609e7   movq (%rcx), %rax
    rcx = 0            the pointer being dereferenced
    rax = 0x80004002   E_NOINTERFACE, the HRESULT the call just returned
    rdx = 0x140dacf70  -> {33AE5EA6-4316-436F-8DDD-D73D22F829EC} = IID_IMF2DBuffer2
    r8  = a stack address, the ppv out-parameter

Those are the arguments of QueryInterface(this, riid, ppv) in the x64 calling
convention, still in place when the fault hit. The game does not check the
HRESULT. A module dump taken inside the fault handler, at the instant of the
fault, showed winegstreamer.dll among the loaded modules, which is what places
the buffer in this source rather than somewhere else.

The refusal comes from mfplat: memory_buffer_QueryInterface (dlls/mfplat/buffer.c)
answers only IID_IMFMediaBuffer and IID_IUnknown and returns E_NOINTERFACE for
everything else. The 1D/2D buffer in the same file would have answered.

WHY 0008 DOES NOT COVER IT

winevideo's 0008, "winegstreamer: always provide 2D-capable output samples on
macOS", is this same fault, found through UE Electra, and its comment states the
mechanism plainly:

    the sample allocator's system-memory buffers implement IMF2DBuffer, which
    consumers like UE Electra require on video frames (a caller-provided plain
    memory buffer does not, so they reject every frame)

But 0008 changes dlls/winegstreamer/video_decoder.c -- the decoder MFT. A title
whose video arrives through winegstreamer's MEDIA SOURCE never goes near it: the
media source produces its own samples in media_stream_send_sample, which called
MFCreateMemoryBuffer and so handed out exactly the buffer 0008 exists to avoid.
Any title that resolves its own byte stream takes that path. This patch applies
the same idea one file over.

WHAT IT DOES

For video streams the buffer now comes from MFCreateMediaBufferFromMediaType,
which returns a 2D-capable buffer when the media type carries a subtype and a
frame size, and falls back to a 1-D buffer of min_length when it does not --
which is the previous behaviour exactly. Two guards ride along:

  - If the locked linear view turns out SMALLER than what the parser produced,
    that frame goes back on a plain buffer rather than overrunning the
    allocation. The two are negotiated together and should agree; this is for
    the case where they do not.
  - If it is LARGER, the tail would never be written, and a fresh allocation is
    not blank. It is filled with black -- luma 0, chroma 128 -- rather than left
    as whatever the heap held. Zeros would be bright green in YUV.

Audio is deliberately left on MFCreateMemoryBuffer: it has no geometry, nothing
asks it for IMF2DBuffer2, and the audio branch of
MFCreateMediaBufferFromMediaType computes a length of its own that there is no
reason to disturb here.

WHAT WAS MEASURED, AND WHAT WAS NOT

With this patch the title plays its cutscenes: eleven minutes of play, two
separate Media Foundation sessions, two different video files, zero exceptions,
where before it died on the first frame of the first one.

Nineteen titles that use Media Foundation, DirectShow or neither were exercised
on an engine carrying this patch, and no failure was traced to it. That is
weaker than it sounds and is stated plainly: the graphics toolkit generation was
not pinned per launch during that pass, so no individual result is tied to a
known toolkit.

A green band along the bottom of the frame in some of that title's cutscenes is
NOT caused by this patch, though an earlier version of this message said it was.
Measured from inside the process: the video is I420 1920x1080 and the buffer
reports current 3110400, max 3110400, contiguous 3110400 -- three equal lengths,
so there is no unwritten tail for the fill above to touch. The plain buffer this
patch replaces was allocated at exactly the same size, so the bytes reaching the
application are identical either way; only the buffer's type changed. The band
appears in some cutscenes and not others, and nothing in this path distinguishes
one from another. Before this patch nobody had seen a frame of that title at
all, because it crashed on the first one.

